package store

import "context"

const businessWebsiteKenyaContent = `
<p>Many business owners know they need a website, but the process can quickly become confusing. Should you buy a .co.ke or .com domain? What kind of hosting do you need? How many pages should the website have? Who writes the content? How do you make sure the site appears on Google? And once the website is launched, who keeps it secure and updated?</p>
<p>These questions matter because a business website is more than a collection of attractive pages. A good website should help people understand your business, trust what you offer, find the information they need, and take the next step — whether that means calling, sending a WhatsApp message, requesting a quotation, booking an appointment, visiting your location, or making a purchase.</p>
<p>For businesses in Kenya, a professional website can also reduce overdependence on social media and referrals by giving the business a digital platform it actually controls.</p>

<h2>What Is a Business Website Supposed to Do?</h2>
<p>Before choosing colours, layouts, hosting packages, or website platforms, start with a more important question: <strong>What should this website help the business achieve?</strong></p>
<p>A business website should explain what the business does, show who it serves, build credibility, present products or services clearly, answer customer questions, make the business easier to find online, and give visitors a clear way to make contact. For some businesses, the main goal is generating enquiries. For others, it may be selling products, attracting investors, receiving applications, showcasing previous work, providing support, or helping people find a physical location.</p>
<p>A company selling construction services in Nairobi will need a different website from a restaurant, consultancy, ecommerce brand, school, SACCO, NGO, or technology startup. Website strategy should come before website design.</p>

<h2>1. Start With Website Planning</h2>
<p>One of the most common website mistakes is starting with design before deciding what the website needs to communicate. Good planning defines the goal, audience, materials, page structure, and next action before development begins.</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/planning-flow.png" alt="Website planning flow from business goal to audience, content and action" loading="lazy"></p>
<h3>Define the main website goal</h3>
<p>Start by identifying the most important action you want visitors to take: request a quotation, call the business, send a WhatsApp message, book a consultation, visit the shop, make a purchase, download a brochure, apply for a programme, or register for an event. A website can have several goals, but one or two should be clearly prioritised.</p>
<h3>Understand your audience</h3>
<p>Consider who will visit the website and what they need to know before taking action. They may be asking: Can this company solve my problem? Do they provide the service I need? Are they based near me? Can I trust them? What experience do they have? How do I get started?</p>
<h3>Gather your materials early</h3>
<p>Collect logo files, brand colours and fonts, company information, product or service details, contact information, photography, testimonials, certifications, client logos, portfolio projects, frequently asked questions, and relevant policies before design starts.</p>

<h2>2. Choose the Right Domain Name</h2>
<p>Your domain is the address people use to access your website. A strong domain should be simple, memorable, easy to type, and closely connected to your business name.</p>
<h3>.co.ke vs .com</h3>
<p>A <strong>.co.ke</strong> domain clearly communicates a Kenyan connection and can be useful for businesses mainly serving the Kenyan market. A <strong>.com</strong> domain may make sense for businesses with a broader regional or international audience. Some businesses register both and redirect one domain to the other.</p>
<p>Look for a domain that is short, easy to spell, easy to say aloud, related to your brand, free from unnecessary hyphens or numbers, and flexible enough for future growth. Keep ownership under an account and email address controlled by the business.</p>

<h2>3. Choose Reliable Website Hosting</h2>
<p>If your domain is the address of your website, hosting is where the website itself lives. Hosting affects website speed, reliability, security, backups, storage, email, support, and the ability to handle increased traffic.</p>
<p>Look for reliable uptime, SSL certificate support, regular backups, adequate storage, good technical support, appropriate server resources, security features, easy website management, and a clear upgrade path. Choose hosting based on what the website actually requires rather than price alone.</p>

<h2>4. Decide What Pages the Website Needs</h2>
<p>A business website should give visitors enough information to understand the business without making them search through unnecessary pages.</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/website-sitemap.png" alt="Business website sitemap showing home, about, services, work, insights and contact pages" loading="lazy"></p>
<h3>Homepage</h3>
<p>The homepage should quickly answer four questions: Who are you? What do you offer? Who do you help? What should the visitor do next?</p>
<p>Avoid vague statements such as “Building tomorrow’s possibilities through innovative solutions.” A clearer opening might say: “Commercial cleaning services for offices, schools, apartments, and retail businesses in Nairobi.”</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/homepage-clarity-comparison.png" alt="Comparison of a vague homepage layout and a clear customer-focused homepage layout" loading="lazy"></p>
<h3>About, services, products and work</h3>
<p>An About page should build confidence through experience, values, team members, areas served, qualifications, and what makes the approach different. Each important service deserves enough space to explain what it involves, who it is for, the problem it solves, the process, expected outcomes, and how to enquire.</p>
<p>Product pages should include descriptions, prices where appropriate, specifications, availability, delivery, payment, returns, and a clear purchase action. Portfolio and case-study pages should show the client’s challenge, what the business provided, the process, images, and the outcome. Never invent projects or testimonials.</p>
<h3>FAQ, blog and contact</h3>
<p>FAQs can remove uncertainty about pricing, timelines, locations, delivery, payment, process, guarantees, requirements, and support. A blog or insights section can help potential customers discover the business through search while demonstrating expertise. Make contact easy with phone, email, WhatsApp, physical location, working hours, a sensible form, and a map where appropriate.</p>

<h2>5. Design the Website Around Clarity</h2>
<p>Good website design is not simply about making the site beautiful. Design should help visitors understand information and move through the website easily. Think about hierarchy, spacing, typography, colour, images, navigation, buttons, mobile layouts, and visual consistency.</p>
<p>Keep the main navigation simple, make headings easy to scan, and give important actions visual priority. Use consistent colours, fonts, photography, icons, button styles, graphic elements, and tone of voice. Use real, high-quality photography where possible, especially for hotels, restaurants, schools, real estate, construction, manufacturing, retail, and professional services.</p>

<h2>6. Write Website Copy Before Filling Pages With Text</h2>
<p>A beautiful website cannot compensate for unclear messaging. Strong copy answers practical customer questions, focuses on the customer’s situation, and replaces generic claims with specific explanations.</p>
<p>Instead of “We offer reliable services,” explain what reliability means. Instead of “We provide quality solutions,” describe the actual solution. Every important page should make the next step clear: Request a Quote, Book a Consultation, Call Our Team, Send Us a WhatsApp Message, or Visit Our Showroom.</p>

<h2>7. Build SEO Into the Website From the Beginning</h2>
<p>Search engine optimisation helps search engines understand your website and helps relevant customers discover it. Start with what customers search for, such as “website design company in Nairobi,” “accounting services in Kenya,” or “solar installation company Nairobi.”</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/seo-checklist.png" alt="SEO checklist with structure, titles, content, speed, mobile, links and image optimisation" loading="lazy"></p>
<p>Basic on-page SEO should include clear page titles, useful meta descriptions, logical headings, descriptive URLs, internal links, image alt text, optimised images, relevant page copy, mobile-friendly layouts, fast loading, and a clear site structure. For local businesses, make service areas clear and maintain a properly managed Google Business Profile.</p>

<h2>8. Make Website Speed a Priority</h2>
<p>People do not enjoy waiting for websites. Huge image files, too many plugins, poor hosting, heavy animations, unnecessary scripts, auto-loading videos, and bloated themes are common causes of slow sites.</p>
<p>Upload images at appropriate dimensions and compress them before publishing. Modern formats such as WebP can reduce file sizes. Use animation with purpose and put performance before spectacle.</p>

<h2>9. Design for Mobile From the Beginning</h2>
<p>A mobile-responsive website adjusts to different screen sizes. Customers may first discover a business through Instagram, TikTok, Google, WhatsApp, email, or a recommendation shared through their phone.</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/mobile-responsive.png" alt="The same business website adapting across desktop, tablet and phone screens" loading="lazy"></p>
<p>Check that text is readable, buttons are easy to tap, menus and forms work, images resize correctly, tables do not break the layout, phone numbers and WhatsApp links are tappable, important content is visible, and pages load quickly. Test on multiple mobile devices rather than approving only a desktop view.</p>

<h2>10. Protect the Website With Proper Security</h2>
<p>At a minimum, a business website should use HTTPS with a valid SSL certificate. Other measures may include strong passwords, two-factor authentication, regular software updates, secure hosting, malware protection, login protection, controlled administrator access, regular backups, spam protection, secure forms, and trusted plugins.</p>
<p>Keep track of access to domain registration, hosting, the CMS, business email, analytics, search tools, and payment accounts. Remove old access when people no longer work on the website.</p>

<h2>11. Plan Website Maintenance Before Launch</h2>
<p>A website is not finished forever on launch day. Businesses change, software needs updating, customers ask new questions, and search behaviour changes.</p>
<p>A maintenance plan can include software and plugin updates, security checks, backups, broken-link checks, form testing, content updates, image optimisation, performance checks, SEO improvements, and analytics review. Decide whether the internal team, website company, or both will be responsible.</p>

<h2>12. Build the Website to Convert Visitors Into Action</h2>
<p>Traffic alone is not the goal. Conversion means helping the right visitor take the intended action.</p>
<p>Build trust with real client work, testimonials, company history, team members, certifications, a physical address, professional photographs, partner logos, case studies, clear contact information, and transparent policies. Use genuine trust signals only, place calls to action where they fit the visitor’s intent, and remove unnecessary friction from forms, enquiries, bookings, and purchases.</p>

<h2>A Practical Business Website Framework</h2>
<p>A useful way to think about your website is through eight connected layers: strategy, structure, content, design, development, discovery, conversion, and maintenance.</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/eight-layer-framework.png" alt="Eight connected layers of an effective business website: strategy, structure, content, design, development, discovery, conversion and maintenance" loading="lazy"></p>
<p>If one layer is ignored, the others have to work harder. Beautiful design cannot rescue confusing content. Good SEO cannot rescue an untrustworthy website. Strong copy cannot rescue a site that barely loads on mobile.</p>

<h2>Building a Website for the Kenyan Business Environment</h2>
<p>Many businesses still grow through referrals, WhatsApp conversations, social media, physical relationships, and repeat customers. A website should support those channels rather than pretend they do not exist.</p>
<p>A potential customer might hear about a business from a friend, search its name on Google, open the website to check credibility, review services and previous work, click WhatsApp, and ask for a quotation. The website may never directly make the sale, but it plays an important role in validating the recommendation and building confidence.</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/conversion-journey.png" alt="Customer journey from referral or search through website, trust and enquiry to sale" loading="lazy"></p>

<h2>Common Website Mistakes Kenyan Businesses Should Avoid</h2>
<ul><li>Starting with design instead of strategy</li><li>Copying a competitor’s website</li><li>Writing vague copy</li><li>Building only for desktop</li><li>Ignoring SEO until later</li><li>Choosing hosting only by price</li><li>Forgetting maintenance</li><li>Having no clear call to action</li></ul>

<h2>Kuza Kizazi Perspective</h2>
<p>At Kuza Kizazi, we believe the strongest business websites are built from the inside out. Start with the business goal, clarify the audience, organise the information, write the message, design the experience, develop the technology, optimise for search and speed, then maintain and improve it based on how people actually use it.</p>
<p>A growing SME might need a focused five-page site with excellent copy, professional photography, local SEO, and clear enquiry options. A retailer may need ecommerce. A school may need admissions information, curriculum pages, news, events, downloadable resources, and parent communication. The right website is the one that helps the right audience understand you, trust you, find what they need, and take the next step.</p>

<h2>Before You Build Your Website</h2>
<p>Do not begin by asking, “Which template should we use?” Start by asking, <strong>“What does the business need this website to accomplish?”</strong></p>
<p>Your website should make the business easier to understand, easier to trust, easier to find, and easier to contact.</p>
<p><img src="/images/blog/business-website-kenya/cta-visual.png" alt="Business website call to action visual with an orange action button and upward arrow" loading="lazy"></p>
<p>If you are planning a new business website or replacing one that no longer represents where your company is going, Kuza Kizazi can help you define the right structure, sharpen the message, and build a website designed around your actual business goals.</p>
`

// seedBusinessWebsiteGuide keeps this editorial post in the deployable backend
// rather than requiring a manual admin-console step after each deployment.
func (s *Store) seedBusinessWebsiteGuide(ctx context.Context) error {
	var categoryID int64
	if err := s.pool.QueryRow(ctx, `
		INSERT INTO categories (name, slug) VALUES ('Websites & Digital Platforms', 'websites-digital-platforms')
		ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET name = EXCLUDED.name
		RETURNING id`).Scan(&categoryID); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	var authorID *int64
	var id int64
	if err := s.pool.QueryRow(ctx, `SELECT id FROM users WHERE role = 'admin' ORDER BY id LIMIT 1`).Scan(&id); err == nil {
		authorID = &id
	}

	_, err := s.pool.Exec(ctx, `
		INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, excerpt, content, cover_image, status, category_id, author_id, published_at)
		VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4, $5, 'published', $6, $7, now())
		ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
			title = EXCLUDED.title,
			excerpt = EXCLUDED.excerpt,
			content = EXCLUDED.content,
			cover_image = EXCLUDED.cover_image,
			status = 'published',
			category_id = EXCLUDED.category_id,
			updated_at = now(),
			published_at = COALESCE(posts.published_at, EXCLUDED.published_at)`,
		"The Complete Guide to Building a Business Website in Kenya",
		"business-website-kenya",
		"Learn how to build a professional business website in Kenya, from planning, domains and hosting to design, SEO, security, maintenance and conversion.",
		businessWebsiteKenyaContent,
		"/images/blog/business-website-kenya/featured-cover.png",
		categoryID,
		authorID,
	)
	return err
}
